  "Question": "A store owner bought \\(1500\\) pencils at \\(\\$0.10\\) each. If he sells them for \\(\\$0.25\\) each, how many of them must he sell to make a profit of exactly \\(\\$100.00?\\)",
  "Question Type": "text",
  "Choices": {
    "A": "\\(400\\)",
    "B": "\\(667\\)",
    "C": "\\(1000\\)",
    "D": "\\(1500\\)",
    "E": "\\(1900\\)"
  },
  "Choices Type": "text",
  "Correct Answer": "C",
  "Solution": "The owner has already paid for all \\(1500\\) pencils. His total purchase cost is\n\\[\n1500\\times\\$0.10=\\$150.\n\\]\nTo recover that cost and make a profit of exactly \\(\\$100\\), he needs sales totaling \\(\\$150+\\$100=\\$250\\).\n\nAt \\(\\$0.25\\) per pencil, the required number sold is\n\\[\n\\frac{250}{0.25}=1000.\n\\]\nThis is within the \\(1500\\) pencils available, and the sales exceed the total purchase cost by exactly \\(\\$100\\). The answer is \\(\\boxed{1000}\\) pencils, choice \\(\\boxed{\\text{C}}\\).",
